From 8d09368da7ecb54d6faed11087c108207e5970cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dugs <dugahol@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 09:56:08 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 04/19] input: apple-z2: D21 calibration encoding, power state,
 and the calibration wait

Adds the D21 (iPhone 8 Plus) report stream work. Touch still does not work:
with the correct firmware, correct calibration, the high-level state Active
and the scan engine demonstrably running (its timing counters advance), a
finger produces no report and no interrupt from the controller. Everything
below was needed to get this far and is verified, not speculative.

Calibration packet length. The HBPP14 header stores the payload length as the
number of four-byte words less one: Apple's own container for this part
carries 15897 for a 63592-byte firmware payload and 4327 for 17312, and the
equivalent converter for the iPad part does the same for calibration packets.
The blobs are stored in Apple's profile with the plain word count, so the
container we build subtracts one; the packet built here did not, which made
the controller read past the payload into the trailing checksum and reject
every calibration blob. The blobs also have to be handed over 16-bit
word-swapped, which this path already did.

Container ordering and the calibration wait. Apple's loader does "requesting
calibration", then "Waiting for calibration to end", then "sending calibration
bytes": the boot sequence ends in a RequestCalibration action, the controller
raises an edge, and only then does the host send the per-device calibration.
Our wait on the boot completion never re-armed it, so a completion left over
from an earlier phase satisfied the wait immediately and the calibration went
out before it was asked for. Re-arm before every wait, complete on every edge,
and log (rather than abort) a missing edge on this part.

High-level power state. Report 0x72 carries a one-byte "Multitouch high level"
state at offset 1 - Unknown, Active, Ready, Rsv, UILock, FaceDet, Rsv, Rsv,
PreReady, Anticip, Diag. The controller reads it back as Unknown until the host
sets it, so declare the UI live from the initialisation path.

Diagnostics behind probe_reports=1: sample the state and counter reports, walk
the firmware's whole report table, and watch any report for changes. Reading a
report pulls it out of the firmware's table and swallows what the interrupt
channel had queued, so this stays off unless asked for. The table walk shows
0x44 (which the iPhone-family parse path treats as a touch report) is really a
static channel-map table that never changes under a finger.

The firmware container is per-device and lives on the device; it must be built
from the personality the unit selects in "hid-merge-personality" (C1F5E,4 for
D21, firmware 0x0773). Run the other one (C1F5E,3, 0x0753) and the controller
still answers, but with the wrong panel profile.
